EXACTLY WHEN EXECUTION BRANCHES.

An English Major C architecture document.
Status: experimental control-flow specification; no executed fixture yet.
Applies to the closed initial ARMv7-A T32 operation grant, especially its
operations 14 through 16 and 42 through 51. This file DOES NOT grant any
new instruction family. An instruction's legality remains constrained
by ARM DDI 0406C.d and by its exact encoding, operands and IT context.
Secondary reference: Arm 100076_0200_00_en, C1.3-C1.10,
C2.14, C2.19-C2.23, C2.41, and C2.154.

PART ONE. WHAT COUNTS AS A CONTROL TRANSFER.

The architectural instruction at address A has length L, where L is
either two or four bytes in this T32 state. Its sequential successor
is A plus L. This remains true when arithmetic is skipped because of
a false condition, provided normal conditional non-execution applies.

A control transfer is an instruction whose permitted effects
replace that sequential successor with an instruction address T,
or, in the case of an exception, transfer to the architectural
exception machinery with separately defined effects.

An architectural branch has no magical register of labels.
A branch encoding may contain a signed PC-relative displacement,
a register target, or a memory-table lookup that provides an
unsigned offset. "The named instruction" in a source description
must be resolved to an actual valid destination and encoding.

PART TWO. THE FLAG CONDITIONS, SPELLED OUT IN ENGLISH.

An instruction that uses the ordinary ARM condition codes consults
the CURRENT values of N, Z, C, and V according to the selected
condition. These are one-bit architectural flags.
The following propositions are the exact required integer
conditions, without any assumption about the direction of a
physical branch predictor:

EQUAL: the zero flag is one. This corresponds to EQ.
NOT EQUAL: the zero flag is zero. This corresponds to NE.
CARRY SET OR UNSIGNED HIGHER OR SAME: carry is one. Corresponds to CS/HS.
CARRY CLEAR OR UNSIGNED LOWER: carry is zero. Corresponds to CC/LO.
NEGATIVE: the negative flag is one. Corresponds to MI.
NONNEGATIVE RESULT: the negative flag is zero. Corresponds to PL.
SIGNED OVERFLOW: the overflow flag is one. Corresponds to VS.
NO SIGNED OVERFLOW: the overflow flag is zero. Corresponds to VC.
UNSIGNED HIGHER: carry is one AND zero is zero. Corresponds to HI.
UNSIGNED LOWER OR SAME: carry is zero OR zero is one. Corresponds to LS.
SIGNED GREATER OR EQUAL: N and V have the same value. Corresponds to GE.
SIGNED LESS: N and V have different values. Corresponds to LT.
SIGNED GREATER: Z is zero AND N and V have the same value. Corresponds to GT.
SIGNED LESS OR EQUAL: Z is one OR N and V have different values.
  Corresponds to LE.
ALWAYS: unconditional when supported by the selected instruction
  encoding. Corresponds to AL where that encoding permits it.

The four-bit code commonly called NV, with its encoding-dependent
special meanings, is NOT an extra everyday "never branch" predicate
in this first profile. Reserved and special encodings must not
be translated as ordinary always-false conditions.

These propositions are conditional operations on flags, NOT
general booleans capable of testing arbitrary memory or user
variables without first generating the correct flags or zero
register predicate.

PART THREE. WHY ONE COMPARISON CAN HAVE TWO DIFFERENT ORDERS.

A subtraction-based comparison sets flags for FIRST MINUS SECOND.

If the first value equals the second, Z is one.

For an UNSIGNED comparison of first and second, C is one when
the subtraction does not borrow. Thus unsigned first higher
than second is C=1 AND Z=0. Unsigned first lower than second
is C=0.

For a SIGNED comparison of first and second, N by itself
does not always indicate signed less-than because signed
subtraction can overflow. Signed first less than second is
the proposition that N and V differ.

This means the following source sentences are NOT equivalent:

"Branch when the first operand is smaller as a signed integer."
"Branch when the first operand is smaller as an unsigned integer."

If first = 0xFFFFFFFF (the signed integer -1) and
second = 0x00000001 (the signed integer +1), then
signed first < second is true but unsigned first < second
is false. The chosen comparison order and interpretation
must be written into the operation's preconditions.

If any intervening instruction changes relevant condition
flags, an earlier comparison is no longer adequate evidence
for a later conditional branch. Programs must either prove
that flags survive or recompute them.

PART FOUR. HOW A CONDITIONALLY TAKEN DIRECT BRANCH WORKS.

The short name BEQ is not the English Major C name of an operation.
A canonical long instruction request is:

"Replace the execution index with the address of instruction
TARGET only if the zero condition flag is one, and otherwise
advance it by the actual length of this conditional branch
instruction, without modifying any general register or any
condition flag."

This is one instance of permitted operation 43. The operation's
operand includes an actual encoded displacement within the
range of the selected T32 branch encoding. It is NOT enough
to say "somewhere near this instruction".

An architectural condition is evaluated using the flags at
the instruction's evaluation point. If it passes, the
execution index becomes the branch target. If it fails, the
execution index becomes A+L. Neither case changes the
condition flags merely by evaluating the branch.

Illustrative address arithmetic, not an assembled fixture:
Given a 16-bit conditional B instruction beginning at
0x00003000, the ordinary sequential successor is 0x00003002.
Its ordinary PC-relative base is 0x00003004. If the encoding
represents a displacement of eight bytes, the selected
branch target is 0x0000300C. When Z=1 for an EQ branch,
execution proceeds from 0x00003000 to 0x0000300C. With
Z=0 it proceeds from 0x00003000 to 0x00003002. The
instructions at intermediate addresses are not executed
merely because they lie in the numerical interval.

PART FOUR-A. A COMPLETE TWO-BYTE CONDITIONAL-BRANCH DECODE REGION.

The initial operation 43 now pins the exact Thumb T1 conditional
branch encoding for first four bits 1101, next four bits a
condition from 0000 through 1101, and eight displacement bits.
The immediate is an eight-bit signed two's-complement integer,
scaled by two. The resulting displacement is -256 through
+254 bytes, and the branch target is current instruction address
plus FOUR bytes plus the displacement.

**The 16-bit T1 conditional B encoding is NEVER legal inside
an IT block.** ARM DDI 0406C.d A8.8.18 labels it
"Not permitted in IT block" and declares such usage
UNPREDICTABLE. The separate rule permitting a PC-changing
operation in the last IT slot applies only to instructions
whose own exact encoding permits that context. It does not
override the T1 prohibition. T2 and T3/T4 have their own
distinct form-specific rules and must not inherit a
made-up universal branch rule.

The complete 0xD000 through 0xDFFF subtree is provided in
"Every conditional-branch and supervisor-call halfword.english".
All 4096 encodings are also individually recorded in
_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-conditional-branch-and-exception-halfwords.tsv,
including 256 UDF and 256 SVC encodings that are decoded
but UNGRANTED by the current English Major C operation list.

If the source supplies an immediate displacement of ZERO,
the target when taken is the current instruction address
plus four, NOT its sequential successor plus two.
A taken branch and an untaken branch can sometimes have the
same numerical successor (for example D0FF), but their
architectural predicates and branch decisions remain
distinct; their performance may also differ.

The finite condition truth table for all four-flag
combinations and all fourteen T1 conditions is
_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-conditional-branch-flag-truth-table.tsv.
It is data about encoding/flag semantics, NOT a program
which has executed every branch on a CPU.

PART FOUR-B. ALL SHORT B T2 BRANCH ENCODINGS.

A distinct, independently bounded two-byte Thumb instruction
is the B T2 immediate encoding E000..E7FF.
Every one of its 2048 possible eleven-bit immediate
values is enumerated in
_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-unconditional-branch-halfwords.tsv.
Its exact signed-offset, PC+4 and instruction-entry
rules are detailed in
"Every short unconditional branch halfword.english".

The B T2 is not a T1 Dxxx conditional branch.
It has no encoded condition field and does not
change the arithmetic flags or the link register.
Its signed PC-relative displacement ranges from
-2048 to +2046 bytes in even steps.

B T2 can be permitted OUTSIDE IT and as the
LAST instruction slot INSIDE a legal IT block.
In the latter context, the governing IT predicate
determines whether its normal branch effect
occurs. A false IT condition suppresses that
effect and execution advances two bytes instead.
A B T2 placed earlier in IT is UNPREDICTABLE.

A native halfword E000 outside IT can be printed
"B"; the SAME halfword under one-slot IT EQ can
be printed "BEQ". The disassembler is displaying
the effective condition from ITSTATE.
A faithful English translation MUST preserve
the same native halfword E000 and the preceding
actual IT operation. It must not reclassify
the E000 bytes as a D000 T1 conditional B.

The numerical equality of a branch target and
the sequential A+2 address, as for E7FF,
does not prove that no branch operation occurred.
The machine's actual control-transfer effect
and the address selected by that effect are
separate statements.

PART FIVE. HOW A REGULAR BRANCH DIFFERS FROM CALLING.

Operation 42: a direct unconditional B replaces the
execution index with an encoded PC-relative target.
It does not save a return address.

Operation 43: a direct conditional B takes the target
only when its applicable condition is true.

Operation 47: an immediate T32 BL both branches and
sets the link register to the Thumb-state-tagged
return address of the instruction following the call.

Operation 48: BX through a register derives the requested
instruction state from bit zero of that register. An
indirect target with bit zero one denotes Thumb state,
with the actual instruction byte address obtained by
discarding that indication bit. An indirect target
with bit zero zero denotes ARM/A32 state, which this
profile must reject. The special LR register must be
permitted as the source for a RETURN by the explicitly
named operation (see the corrected grant).

Operation 49: BLX through a register similarly sets LR
and takes an indirect target, but only T32 targets
are permitted under this grant.

An ordinary B does not automatically turn into a call
because the destination begins a function. A BL does
not automatically establish a software call frame,
save caller-owned registers, create a stack record,
or unwind an exception. Those are separate conventions.

PART SIX. A ZERO-BASED BRANCH THAT DOES NOT USE THE FLAGS.

Operation 44, CBZ, and operation 45, CBNZ, test whether the
entire specified register is zero or nonzero. They leave
N, Z, C, and V unchanged, rather than performing a
CMP that updates those flags.

They are 16-bit T32 encodings. The branch displacement
is an unsigned multiple of two bytes in the range zero
through 126, added to the instruction's architectural
PC-relative base. They cannot encode a backward branch.
They may not be used inside an IT block. The exact
register encoding must be legal, including the 16-bit
form's limitation to the low general-purpose registers
R0 through R7.

The zero to be tested is a register value, not the
zero condition flag. A data encoding such as R's
NA_INTEGER is not zero merely because the program's
semantic predicate calls it a "missing value". A
preliminary equality comparison, conversion or
bitwise calculation is needed unless the tested value
is genuinely a zero/nonzero predicate.

PART SEVEN. A TABLE LOOKUP THAT CAUSES A BRANCH.

Operation 50 is Table Branch using Byte Entry, TBB.
Operation 51 is Table Branch using Halfword Entry, TBH.
They are 32-bit T32 instructions, not 16-bit T32 forms.

The base register may be a permitted general register
or PC. It must not be SP. The index register is a
general-purpose register and cannot be SP or PC.

For TBB, read a single unsigned byte at:
    [contents of base register] + [contents of index register].
For TBH, read a single unsigned sixteen-bit halfword at:
    [contents of base register] + two times [contents of index register].

When the base register is PC, its value for table-address
formation is A+4, where A is the address of the TBB/TBH
instruction. This is distinct from the current index.

In either form, if the unsigned memory value is K,
the branch target is A+4+(two times K).
The entry width changes the maximum representable K,
not the factor of two used in the branch target.
The table bytes are DATA. Their numerical value
changes the next instruction address; the bytes are
not decoded as T32 instructions in this operation.

An illustrative TBB at A=0x00002002 has PC base
0x00002006. With a base register containing 0x00003000,
index register containing 3, and byte at 0x00003003
equal to unsigned integer 5, the table branch goes to
0x00002010. For TBH with the same base and index, a
sixteen-bit integer 5 stored at address 0x00003006
also produces destination 0x00002010.

The machine instruction contains no abstract bound
on the software table's logical number of entries.
The program must establish that index 3 is a permitted
logical index before using that indexed read in a
checked program. A memory-mapped index outside the
intended table might read something else or fault;
it must not be silently interpreted as a safe
high-level switch statement.

TBB and TBH are not permitted inside an IT block.
An offset entry is unsigned, so these instructions
naturally express PC-relative forward dispatch.
Their branch cannot be assumed to select A32 state.

PART EIGHT. CONDITIONAL EXECUTION WITHOUT AN ORDINARY BRANCH.

Operation 46 is T32 IT: If-Then conditional execution.
An IT instruction determines conditions for one through
four following instruction slots. The first is governed
by the initial condition, and later slots may use that
condition or its inverse according to the legal mask.

The machine must track the condition state and know
when it has been consumed. A conditional instruction
whose condition fails is still occupying its real
two-byte or four-byte location in the T32 instruction
stream. The corresponding effects are suppressed
according to architectural rules. The next sequential
address advances past the entire encoded instruction.

Nesting IT is not permitted. CBZ, CBNZ, TBB and TBH
cannot be placed in an IT block. An instruction
changing PC is allowed only in the last slot of
a legal IT block. A normal branch into the middle
of an active IT block is not permitted by the
checked source model. Arm's exception-return cases
must be handled by the full architecture, not
fabricated as ordinary branch entry.

Do not claim that any 16-bit instruction can be
placed in any IT slot: certain encodings behave
differently outside IT, including flag-setting
forms, and some combinations are disallowed
or deprecated. In particular, a software model
must not rely on the mnemonic alone to infer
whether an arithmetic operation updates flags.

The 2019 Arm assembly guide cautions that multi-slot
IT usage is deprecated in Armv8. This document
targets ARMv7-A, and it must neither retroactively
remove legal ARMv7 forms nor assume they are
advisable for every future AArch32 target.

PART EIGHT-A. EVERY IT AND HINT ENCODING, WITH ALL RESTRICTIONS.

The other closed finite T32 control subgroup is
BF00..BFFF, sixteen-bit Thumb IT and hint operations.
It is exhaustively specified in
"Every If Then and hint halfword.english",
and its 256 individual bit patterns are recorded in
_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-if-then-and-hint-halfwords.tsv.

If the low four bits of a BFxx halfword equal zero,
the machine decodes a hint, NOT an IT instruction.
BF00 is the permitted NOP. BF10/20/30/40 decode
as YIELD/WFE/WFI/SEV respectively, but are NOT
granted source operations. Eleven remaining BFx0
forms are unallocated reserved hints; they may
behave as NOP but ARM says software MUST NOT use them.

If the low mask is nonzero, the instruction is in
IT's encoding family. The four-bit firstcond F
makes the encoding UNPREDICTABLE. A firstcond E
is allowed only when the mask has exactly one set
bit; the other masks in that firstcond group are
UNPREDICTABLE. IT cannot be nested inside IT.

This finite decode identifies 214 conditionally
admissible IT encodings, 26 unpredictable encodings,
one permitted NOP, four ungranted named hints, and
eleven reserved software-prohibited hints.

An IT instruction counts following INSTRUCTIONS,
not following bytes. A two-byte MOV and a four-byte
MOVW are TWO instruction slots, occupying SIX bytes
of following instruction stream. The ITSTATE changes
which data-register effects occur, while ordinary
sequential execution can visit exactly the same
instruction addresses for different flag states.

This is why even an executable English operation
with an elaborate human explanation must distinguish
ITSTATE effects from a branch replacing the execution
index. A disassembler printing "it al" is not
sufficient legality evidence: LLVM 17 displays
both BFE8 (potentially legal IT AL) and BFF8
(architecturally UNPREDICTABLE firstcond F) that
way. Source semantics must check raw encoded bits.

The source-derived byte-level example is:
examples/One If Then block governs a short and a long instruction.english

PART EIGHT-B. AN INSTRUCTION INSIDE IT THAT IGNORES THE PREDICATE.

The machine also has BKPT T1 encodings BE00..BEFF,
which produce architecture-defined breakpoint
debug events. The complete 256-halfword decode
is recorded in
"Every breakpoint halfword and its
conditional-execution exception.english",
and the data-only row inventory is:
_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-breakpoint-halfwords.tsv.

BKPT is ALWAYS UNCONDITIONAL, including when
it appears inside an active IT block whose
condition is false. Therefore, an ordinary
source assumption that "every instruction
inside IT has suppressed effects when its
predicate fails" is INCORRECT.

The initial English Major C source profile
does not grant BKPT execution at all.
The architecture can decode the bytes,
but executing them would request a debug
event with target-dependent halt/exception
consequences. No ordinary successful
execution-index successor is asserted
without an explicit debug environment.

A T1 conditional B in IT is prohibited;
a B T2 in its final IT slot is conditionally
executed; BKPT in IT is unconditional.
These are three different rules applied
to different actual native instruction
encodings. The word "conditional" cannot
be assigned to an instruction merely
because it is textually inside an IT block.

PART NINE. THE EXECUTION-INDEX INVARIANTS.

The next instruction address is one of the
architecturally permitted successors of the exact
decoded operation, not an arbitrary character offset
in a plain-English source file.

A successor is valid in the checked English representation
only if the referenced target can be decoded as an
authorized instruction entry, the required state is
T32, and its access is executable in the target
environment. The raw CPU can sometimes decode bytes
starting at a halfword address not chosen by the source
program. This checked-language restriction does not
imply hardware keeps a protected list of instruction
boundaries.

A branch does not normally change N, Z, C, or V.
A flag-setting compare does not by itself change
the execution index except sequentially. A BL
overwrites LR. CBZ/CBNZ do not consume or update
the comparison flags. TBB/TBH do not execute
their table's bytes.

A failed condition and an actual fault are different
outcomes, and neither can be replaced by a prose
comment saying that an instruction was skipped.

PART TEN. COMPLETE NEGATIVE CASES TO RETAIN.

A verifier must reject a deliberately incorrect
translation for each of these distinct defects:
  * confusing signed and unsigned less-than;
  * reversing the operands of a comparison;
  * using stale flags after a flag-modifying operation;
  * changing an untaken branch into a taken branch;
  * incrementing the execution index by four after
    a two-byte instruction;
  * interpreting PC operand as the sequential index
    after a two-byte instruction;
  * clearing the Thumb-state bit before recording LR;
  * attempting an A32 BX or BLX target without an
    explicitly permitted separate architecture grant;
  * assuming that CBZ reads Z rather than the register;
  * emitting a backward CBZ or CBNZ;
  * emitting CBZ/CBNZ inside an IT block;
  * emitting IT inside another IT block;
  * placing a PC-changing instruction before the
    last slot of an IT block;
  * interpreting the table value as a byte address
    rather than twice a halfword displacement;
  * using byte-table indexing for a halfword table;
  * treating a TBB table byte as an instruction;
  * loading an invalid table entry and pretending
    the machine checked high-level array bounds;
  * claiming that an unsupported ARM mnemonic is
    already authorized by the 73-operation profile;
  * confusing an address's T32-indication bit with
    the least significant bit of the target's address.

No negative case above has yet been run as an
executable, independently checked test. The
existence of this list does not count as acceptance.

END OF THIS CONTROL-FLOW SPECIFICATION.
